## 2. Decentralized Task Allocation: CBBA / ACBBA Formulation

Task allocation is governed by the Consensus-Based Bundle Algorithm (CBBA), executed locally on each robot without a central coordinator.

### 2.1 Agent State Vectors
For each robot $i \in \{1, \dots, N\}$ and task catalog $\mathcal{T} = \{1, \dots, M\}$:
1. **Task Bundle** $b_i = (b_{i,1}, b_{i,2}, \dots, b_{i,|b_i|}) \in \mathcal{T}^{|b_i|}$, where $|b_i| \le L_{\text{max}} = 8$ is the maximum bundle capacity.
2. **Path Waypoint Sequence** $p_i$: The corresponding ordered physical locations $(x, y)$ that robot $i$ must visit to execute bundle $b_i$.
3. **Winning Bid Table** $\mathbf{y}_i \in \mathbb{R}^M$: $y_{ij}$ represents robot $i$'s current belief of the highest bid placed for task $j$.
4. **Winning Agent Table** $\mathbf{z}_i \in \{1, \dots, N, \emptyset\}^M$: $z_{ij}$ represents robot $i$'s current belief of which robot holds task $j$.
5. **Information Timestamp Table** $\mathbf{s}_i \in \mathbb{R}^N$: $s_{ik}$ is the timestamp of the last message received from peer $k$.

### 2.2 Phase 1: Bundle Construction & Marginal Score Computation
Each robot greedily evaluates unallocated candidate tasks $j \in \mathcal{T} \setminus b_i$:

#### Base Priority Reward
Each task $j$ carries priority $P_j \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$ ($\text{LOW}=1, \text{NORMAL}=2, \text{HIGH}=3, \text{CRITICAL}=4$):
$$U_{\text{base}}(j) = w_p \cdot P_j, \quad w_p = 10.0$$

#### Cumulative Travel Distance
For candidate insertion position $m \in \{m_{\text{locked}}, \dots, |b_i|\}$ in bundle $b_i$:
$$\text{cand\_bundle} = (b_{i,1}, \dots, b_{i,m}, j, b_{i,m+1}, \dots, b_{i,|b_i|})$$
The cumulative distance from the robot's current pose $\mathbf{x}_i(t)$ through all predecessors up to task $j$'s dropoff is:
$$\text{cum\_dist}(m) = \sum_{k=1}^m \left( \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{dropoff}, k-1} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{pickup}, k}\|_2 + \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pickup}, k} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{dropoff}, k}\|_2 \right)$$
where $\mathbf{x}_{\text{dropoff}, 0} = \mathbf{x}_i(t)$.

#### Travel & Late Cost Functions
$$C_{\text{travel}}(m) = w_d \cdot \text{cum\_dist}(m), \quad w_d = 1.0$$
$$\text{est\_arrival}(m) = t_{\text{curr}} + \frac{\text{cum\_dist}(m)}{v_{\text{nom}}}$$
$$C_{\text{late}}(m) = \begin{cases} w_{\text{late}} \cdot (\text{est\_arrival}(m) - D_j) & \text{if } D_j > 0 \land \text{est\_arrival}(m) > D_j \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}, \quad w_{\text{late}} = 2.0$$

#### Net Marginal Utility with Geometric Discounting
The marginal utility of task $j$ inserted at position $m$ is:
$$U_{\text{net}}(j, m) = U_{\text{base}}(j) - C_{\text{travel}}(m) - C_{\text{late}}(m)$$
Applying geometric temporal discounting to favor immediate tasks:
$$c_{ij}(m) = \left(\gamma^{|b_i|}\right) \cdot \max\left(0.01, \, U_{\text{net}}(j, m)\right), \quad \gamma = 0.95$$

The optimal insertion position $m^*$ and maximum marginal utility $c_{ij}$ are:
$$c_{ij} = \max_{m \ge m_{\text{locked}}} c_{ij}(m), \quad m^* = \arg\max_{m \ge m_{\text{locked}}} c_{ij}(m)$$

#### Insertion Rule
Task $j$ is inserted into bundle $b_i$ at position $m^*$ if:
$$c_{ij} > y_{ij} + \epsilon, \quad \epsilon = 10^{-4}$$
Upon insertion, robot $i$ updates its local belief:
$$z_{ij} \leftarrow i, \quad y_{ij} \leftarrow c_{ij}$$

### 2.3 Phase 2: Consensus & Conflict Resolution
Robots periodically broadcast their bid vectors $(\mathbf{y}_i, \mathbf{z}_i, \mathbf{s}_i)$ over peer-to-peer ROS 2 gossip topics (`/{robot_id}/cbba_bid`).
Upon receiving an incoming bid packet from neighbor $k$, agent $i$ updates its belief for every task $j$ according to the definitive 11-rule consensus matrix:

| Case | Local State ($z_{ij}$) | Peer State ($z_{kj}$) | Condition | Action Taken by Agent $i$ |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| **1** | $i$ | $k$ | $y_{kj} > y_{ij}$ | **Update**: $z_{ij} \leftarrow k, \, y_{ij} \leftarrow y_{kj}$ (Outbid) |
| **2** | $i$ | $k$ | $y_{kj} \le y_{ij}$ | **Leave**: Retain current ownership |
| **3** | $i$ | $m \ne i, k$ | $y_{kj} > y_{ij} \land s_{km} > s_{im}$ | **Update**: $z_{ij} \leftarrow m, \, y_{ij} \leftarrow y_{kj}$ |
| **4** | $i$ | $m \ne i, k$ | $y_{kj} \le y_{ij} \land s_{km} > s_{im}$ | **Leave**: Local bid is superior |
| **5** | $k$ | $k$ | $s_{kk} > s_{ik}$ | **Update**: Peer refreshed its bid |
| **6** | $k$ | $m \ne i, k$ | $s_{km} > s_{im}$ | **Update**: $z_{ij} \leftarrow m, \, y_{ij} \leftarrow y_{kj}$ |
| **7** | $m \ne i, k$ | $m$ | $s_{km} > s_{im}$ | **Update**: Newer info on third party |
| **8** | $m \ne i, k$ | $k$ | $y_{kj} > y_{ij}$ | **Update**: Peer outbid third party |
| **9** | $m \ne i, k$ | $n \ne i, k, m$ | $s_{kn} > s_{in} \land y_{kj} > y_{ij}$ | **Update**: Newer information |
| **10** | $m \ne i, k$ | $\emptyset$ | $s_{km} > s_{im}$ | **Reset**: $z_{ij} \leftarrow \emptyset, \, y_{ij} \leftarrow 0$ |
| **11** | $\emptyset$ | any | $y_{kj} > 0$ | **Update**: Claim task info |

#### Deterministic Lexicographical Tie-Breaking
When bids are identical within precision tolerance $|y_{ij} - y_{kj}| \le \epsilon$:
$$\text{Winner} = \begin{cases} \text{Agent } i & \text{if } y_{ij} - y_{kj} > \epsilon \\ \text{Agent } k & \text{if } y_{kj} - y_{ij} > \epsilon \\ \text{lexicographical}(\text{ID}_i < \text{ID}_k) & \text{if } |y_{ij} - y_{kj}| \le \epsilon \end{cases}$$

#### Bundle Cascade Pruning Rule
If task $j$ at index $\bar{m}$ in bundle $b_i$ is outbid and surrendered:
$$b_i \leftarrow (b_{i,1}, b_{i,2}, \dots, b_{i,\bar{m}-1})$$
For all vacated downstream tasks $k \in \{b_{i,\bar{m}}, \dots, b_{i,|b_i|}\}$:
$$z_{ik} \leftarrow \emptyset, \quad y_{ik} \leftarrow 0$$
This preserves bundle ordering optimality and avoids phantom sub-routes.

### 2.4 Convergence Metric & Execution Gate
A robot's bundle is mathematically converged when:
$$\text{is\_converged}(i) \iff \forall j \in b_i, \quad z_{ij} = i \quad \land \quad \forall k \in \mathcal{N}_i, \quad s_{ik} \ge t_{\text{last\_bid\_change}}$$
Motion planning execution commits **only** to tasks in converged bundles.

