## 3. Task Sequencing & Bundle Execution Heuristics

Once a robot achieves bundle convergence, `TaskSequencer` orders the execution sequence using one of four deterministic strategies:

### 3.1 PRIORITY_FIRST (Default Operational Heuristic)
Tasks are sorted by descending priority, with distance to robot as secondary tie-breaker:
$$\mathcal{K}_{\text{pri}}(j) = \Big( -P_j, \, \text{round}\big(\|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pickup}, j} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{robot}}\|_2, 3\big), \, \text{task\_id}_j \Big)$$
$$b_i^* = \text{sort}\left(b_i, \, \text{key} = \mathcal{K}_{\text{pri}}\right)$$

### 3.2 SHORTEST_PATH_FIRST (Greedy TSP Routing)
Minimizes total deadhead travel distance via sequential nearest neighbor:
$$\text{Given current anchor pose } \mathbf{x}_{\text{anchor}}:$$
$$j^* = \arg\min_{j \in b_{\text{remaining}}} \Big( \|\mathbf{x}_{\text{pickup}, j} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{anchor}}\|_2, \, -P_j, \, \text{task\_id}_j \Big)$$
$$\mathbf{x}_{\text{anchor}} \leftarrow \mathbf{x}_{\text{dropoff}, j^*}, \quad b_{\text{remaining}} \leftarrow b_{\text{remaining}} \setminus \{j^*\}$$

### 3.3 DEADLINE_FIRST (Earliest Deadline First — EDF)
Prioritizes tight delivery windows:
$$\mathcal{K}_{\text{dl}}(j) = \Big( D_j, \, -P_j, \, \text{task\_id}_j \Big)$$

### 3.4 In-Progress Preservation Invariant
If a robot is actively executing an in-progress task ($t_{\text{active}}$ in phase `TRANSIT_TO_PICKUP` or `TRANSIT_TO_DROPOFF`), dynamic mid-mission bundle re-auctioning freezes index $m_{\text{locked}}$:
$$m_{\text{locked}} = \text{active\_task\_idx} + 1$$
$$b_i^* = (b_{i, 1}, \dots, b_{i, \text{active\_task\_idx}}) \oplus \text{Sequence}\left(b_{i, \text{new}}\right)$$
Active tasks **never abort or reverse** due to mid-mission auctions.

