## 5. Space-Time Corridor Reservations & Conflict Detection

### 5.1 Reservation Table Mathematics
Each robot maintains a local `ReservationTable` containing 4D space-time bounding intervals:
$$R_i = \left\{ \big(x_k, y_k, t_{k, \text{start}}, t_{k, \text{end}}, \text{prio}\big) \right\}$$
where:
$$t_{k, \text{start}} = k \cdot \Delta t, \quad t_{k, \text{end}} = (k + 1) \cdot \Delta t + \tau_{\text{buffer}}, \quad \tau_{\text{buffer}} = 0.20\,\text{s}$$

### 5.2 Dynamic Forward Headway Sizing
To guarantee collision-free corridor stopping under differential-drive braking, each robot reserves forward headway cells along its velocity vector:
$$d_{\text{headway}}(v) = v \cdot t_{\text{reaction}} + \frac{v^2}{2 \cdot a_{\text{decel}}}$$
For nominal speed $v_{\text{nom}} = 0.80\,\text{m/s}$, reaction latency $t_{\text{reaction}} = 0.20\,\text{s}$, and deceleration $a_{\text{decel}} = 1.20\,\text{m/s}^2$:
$$d_{\text{headway}} = (0.80)(0.20) + \frac{0.64}{2(1.20)} = 0.160 + 0.267 = 0.427\,\text{m}$$
In terms of discrete grid cells ($\delta_{\text{grid}} = 0.50\,\text{m}$):
$$N_{\text{headway}} = \left\lceil \frac{d_{\text{headway}}}{\delta_{\text{grid}}} \right\rceil = 1 \text{ forward cell (inflated to 2 cells in narrow corridors)}.$$

### 5.3 Formal Conflict Conditions
A conflict between Robot $A$ and Robot $B$ occurs if any of the three spatio-temporal invariants is breached:
1. **Vertex / Co-occupancy Conflict**:
   $$\exists t \in [t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{end}}] \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \|\mathbf{x}_A(t) - \mathbf{x}_B(t)\|_2 < D_{\text{clear}} = 0.75\,\text{m}$$
2. **Edge / Head-on Swap Conflict**:
   $$\exists t \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \left( \|\mathbf{x}_A(t) - \mathbf{x}_B(t+1)\|_2 < \delta_{\text{grid}} \right) \land \left( \|\mathbf{x}_B(t) - \mathbf{x}_A(t+1)\|_2 < \delta_{\text{grid}} \right)$$
3. **Following / Tailgating Conflict**:
   $$\mathbf{x}_A(t+1) = \mathbf{x}_B(t) \quad \land \quad v_A > v_B \quad \land \quad \|\mathbf{x}_A(t) - \mathbf{x}_B(t)\|_2 < d_{\text{headway}}$$

