## 6. Priority Inheritance Backtracking (PIBT) Local Coordinator

When two robots project overlapping space-time reservations in the immediate next timestep ($t + \Delta t$), PIBT resolves contention locally without centralized replanning.

### 6.1 Priority Vector Formulation
Each robot's dynamic priority tuple is strictly ordered:
$$\mathcal{P}_i(t) = \Big( P_{\text{task}, i}, \, -d_{\text{subgoal}, i}(t), \, -\text{NumericID}(i) \Big)$$
where:
- $P_{\text{task}, i} \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$: Higher task priority strictly dominates.
- $-d_{\text{subgoal}, i}$: Closer robots have higher priority (minimizes transit stalling).
- $-\text{NumericID}(i)$: Deterministic tie-breaker (lower numeric ID wins).

### 6.2 Priority Inheritance Mechanism
If Robot $A$ targets cell $C = \mathbf{x}_A(t+1)$ currently occupied by Robot $B$:
1. If $\mathcal{P}_A > \mathcal{P}_B$:
   - Robot $B$ temporarily inherits priority: $\mathcal{P}_B \leftarrow \mathcal{P}_A$.
   - Robot $B$ evaluates its 4-connected neighbors $\mathcal{N}(C)$ to vacate cell $C$.
   - If an empty adjacent cell $C' \in \mathcal{N}(C)$ is unreserved, Robot $B$ steps to $C'$.
2. If Robot $B$ is completely constrained (all neighbors blocked or occupied by higher-priority agents):
   - Priority inheritance backtracks to Robot $A$.
   - Robot $A$ yields: $\mathbf{x}_A(t+1) \leftarrow \mathbf{x}_A(t)$ (executes a 1-step wait action).

