## 9. Communication Impairment & Stale-State Eviction Dynamics

Real-world wireless channels in warehouse environments are modeled by the active impairment node `CommunicationModelNode`.

### 9.1 Network Impairment Profiles
For any outgoing inter-robot message $M$:

| Parameter | `NORMAL` | `LOSS_LOW` | `LOSS_HIGH` |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| **Packet Loss Ratio ($p_{\text{loss}}$)** | $0.00$ | $0.10$ ($10\%$) | $0.35$ ($35\%$) |
| **Mean Transmission Delay ($\mu_{\text{delay}}$)** | $0.0\,\text{ms}$ | $20.0\,\text{ms}$ | $80.0\,\text{ms}$ |
| **Jitter Standard Deviation ($\sigma_{\text{jitter}}$)** | $0.0\,\text{ms}$ | $5.0\,\text{ms}$ | $20.0\,\text{ms}$ |

#### Stochastic Drop Mechanism
$$X \sim \text{Uniform}(0, 1)$$
$$\text{Action} = \begin{cases} \text{DROP} & \text{if } X < p_{\text{loss}} \\ \text{DELAY } \tau & \text{if } X \ge p_{\text{loss}} \end{cases}$$
where $\tau \sim \max\left(0, \, \mathcal{N}(\mu_{\text{delay}}, \sigma_{\text{jitter}}^2)\right)$.

### 9.2 Stale-State Time-To-Live (TTL) Pruning
When peer packets are dropped, a robot might permanently hold phantom reservations for a disconnected neighbor, leading to false deadlocks.
Each robot's `StaleStateManager` tracks heartbeat timestamps:
$$\Delta t_{\text{peer}}(j) = t_{\text{now}} - t_{\text{last\_heartbeat}}(j)$$
The TTL eviction rule enforces:
$$\text{If } \Delta t_{\text{peer}}(j) > T_{\text{TTL}} = 1.50\,\text{s} \implies \text{Purge all } R_j \text{ from } \text{ReservationTable}$$
Reclaiming stale corridor cells allows healthy AMRs to route around stalled or disconnected peers without human intervention.

